/* A theorem-shaped program: a predicate whose reduction is a proof.

   Turchin's test of a proof (1986 6, *The Concept of a Supercompiler*): "If a
   predicate function P(x) is supercompiled and its configuration graph reduces
   to the single terminal node 'True', this constitutes an automated mathematical
   proof that P(x) holds for all inputs x." The same mechanism is layer 3 of the
   1991 CCNY report *A Supersystem of Language Refal*.

   `Marked` is the theorem: a non-empty text is not empty. Its only sentence
   succeeds for every input that has a head, so every path through its
   configuration graph terminates in the single node 'True'. The prover reports a
   proof for it, and the exit status is zero.

   The claim is deliberately stated the way the corpus states its theorems: a
   predicate over a wholly unknown expression, with the decision inside the
   predicate rather than in the entry. Driving the entry would reduce `Go` and
   leave the predicate invisible -- which is why the prover enters at the
   *predicate* and why `refal prove <file> <Predicate>` takes a name. */

$EXTERN Prout;

$ENTRY Go {
  e.Input = <Prout <Marked e.Input>>;
}

/* The predicate under proof. True whenever the text has a head. */
Marked {
  s.First e.Rest = 'True';
}
